The MarkdownView control was not properly handling data URL images (base64 encoded images), causing them to display as `[Image]` text instead of actual images.

## Problem

When using data URL images in markdown content like:

```markdown
3x ![♥](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAABAAAAAQCAYAAAAf8/9h...)
```

The images would appear as `[Image]` text instead of rendering the actual image:

![Before fix showing [Image] text](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/791f9d59-9ef8-4bf3-8161-4ece39dd8f4b)

## Root Cause

The `LoadImageAsync` method in `MarkdownView.cs` was incorrectly validating the entire data URL as base64:

```csharp
// OLD (broken) logic
if (System.Buffers.Text.Base64.IsValid(imageUrl)) // This fails for data URLs
{
    // Handle as base64
}
else if (Uri.TryCreate(imageUrl, UriKind.Absolute, out Uri uriResult))
{
    // Try to download as web resource (fails for data URLs)
}
```

Data URLs have the format `data:image/png;base64,<base64-content>`, so the entire string is not valid base64 - only the part after `base64,` is.

## Solution

Updated the logic to properly detect and parse data URLs:

```csharp
// NEW (fixed) logic
if (imageUrl.StartsWith("data:", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
{
    var base64StartIndex = imageUrl.IndexOf("base64,");
    if (base64StartIndex != -1)
    {
        var base64Data = imageUrl.Substring(base64StartIndex + "base64,".Length);
        if (System.Buffers.Text.Base64.IsValid(base64Data))
        {
            byte[] imageBytes = Convert.FromBase64String(base64Data);
            imageSource = ImageSource.FromStream(() => new MemoryStream(imageBytes));
        }
    }
}
```

## Changes

- ✅ Added proper data URL detection (`data:` prefix)
- ✅ Extract base64 content after `base64,` marker
- ✅ Validate only the extracted base64 part
- ✅ Maintain backwards compatibility for pure base64 strings
- ✅ Added test cases to sample app demonstrating the fix

## Testing

- Validated with the original issue's data URL
- Tested edge cases (different image formats, invalid base64, etc.)
- Confirmed backwards compatibility with existing functionality
- Added examples to the sample application

Now data URL images render correctly as actual images instead of `[Image]` placeholder text.

Fixes #41.

